+### [Visible Faces Driven Ray Casting](15651-visible-faces-driven-ray-casting.md)
+A memory-efficient variant that reduces memory footprint by storing only the face data of the most recent traversals, exploiting ray coherence among neighboring pixels. It sets itself apart from Exterior-Face Ray Casting by explicitly managing a temporary buffer (`computedFaces`) to cache internal face parameters for the current visible set, thereby avoiding redundant geometric computations for rays traversing the same internal boundaries.
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-
-**Time Complexity:**
-*   Preprocessing: $O(F)$ where $F$ is the number of exterior faces [S1, unofficial][S2, unofficial].
-*   Ray Casting: Proportional to the number of pixels and the average number of elements traversed per ray [S1, unofficial][S2, unofficial].
-*   Compositing: Depends on the number of ray segments per pixel and the sorting cost [S2, unofficial].
-
-**Memory Complexity:**
-*   Ray Buffer: Size depends on the number of ray segments per pixel, which varies with viewing position, data shape, and partitioning [S2, unofficial].
-*   Data Structures: Connectivity and exterior face lists are stored in memory [S1, unofficial][S2, unofficial].
-
-**Measured Performance:**
-*   Overlapping ray casting and compositing can improve overall image compositing performance by up to 40% [S2, unofficial].
-*   Adaptive sampling significantly increases rendering time compared to fixed sampling [S1, unofficial][S2, unofficial].
-
-**Strengths:**
-*   Efficient culling of invisible faces [S1, unofficial][S2, unofficial].
-*   Parallelizable via associative compositing [S1, unofficial][S2, unofficial].
-*   Handles unstructured grids and complex geometries [S1, unofficial][S2, unofficial].
-
-**Limitations:**
-*   Load imbalance due to varying ray traversal lengths [S2, unofficial].
-*   Network congestion if compositing is not overlapped [S2, unofficial].
-*   Complexity of handling concave and partitioned boundaries [S1, unofficial][S2, unofficial].
-
-**When to Use:**
-*   Parallel volume rendering of unstructured grids on distributed-memory architectures [S1, unofficial][S2, unofficial].
-*   When efficient culling of invisible geometry is critical [S1, unofficial][S2, unofficial].
-
-**When Not to Use:**
-*   Interactive rendering rates may require more elaborate image partitioning [S2, unofficial].
+- **Time Complexity:** Dependent on the number of visible exterior faces and the number of elements traversed per ray. Adaptive sampling increases complexity significantly [S1, S2, unofficial].
+- **Memory Complexity:** Requires storage for the exterior face list, visible face list, and the Ray Buffer. The Ray Buffer size grows during rendering and depends on viewing position, data shape, and partitioning [S2, unofficial].
+- **Measured Performance:** Overlapping ray casting and compositing can improve overall image compositing performance by as much as 40% [S2, unofficial].
+- **Scaling:** Load balancing is affected by early ray termination. Subvolumes with larger projected areas of high-density regions (high opacity) cause rays to terminate earlier, traversing fewer elements, leading to uneven load distribution [S2, unofficial].
+- **Strengths:** Efficiently handles unstructured grids with concavities and holes; parallelizable via associative compositing [S1, S2, unofficial].
+- **Limitations:** Adaptive sampling is expensive; Ray Buffer management can be complex; load balancing issues due to variable ray traversal lengths [S1, S2, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-
-**Origin:**
-*   Developed for parallel volume rendering of unstructured-grid data on distributed-memory architectures [S1, unofficial][S2, unofficial].
-*   First described in 1996 [S1, unofficial][S2, unofficial].
-
-**History and Development:**
-*   The method builds on the principles of volume ray casting and parallel rendering [S1, unofficial][S2, unofficial].
-*   It addresses the challenges of irregular shapes, concavities, and holes in unstructured domains, which are exacerbated by data partitioning [S1, unofficial][S2, unofficial].
